WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Two results now meet. Delta
of f is an algebraic integer,

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.027
and every algebraic conjugate
has absolute value one.

00:00:08.473 --> 00:00:11.820
Kronecker's theorem says that these
two facts force a root of unity.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.600
To see why, call this number z, and
let d be its degree over the rationals.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.880
Algebraic integrality
means that z satisfies

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.340
a monic polynomial with
integer coefficients.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.220
Take any positive integer m. The power z
to the m is still an algebraic integer.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.860
Its degree is at most d, and every
conjugate still has absolute value one.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.040
Write its monic minimal polynomial
with degree r, where r is at most d.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.920
We will bound its integer
coefficients independently of m.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.740
Start with a schematic degree
three picture. Its three roots,

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.540
alpha, beta, and gamma,
each have magnitude one.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.600
Expand the product of X minus each root.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.200
Up to signs, the coefficients
are alpha plus beta plus gamma;

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.120
alpha beta plus alpha gamma plus
beta gamma; and alpha beta gamma.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.680
Every product has magnitude one.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.467
The triangle inequality bounds the
three coefficient magnitudes by three,

00:01:13.853 --> 00:01:17.860
three, and one. In
degree r, the coefficient

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.180
k places below the leading term is a
sum of products of k distinct roots.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.940
There are r choose k such
terms, each of magnitude one.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.380
Its magnitude is at most r choose
k, and hence at most d choose k.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.200
This bound does not depend on m.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.100
Now use the integer condition.
For each possible degree,

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.740
every coefficient is an
integer in a bounded range.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.380
The leading coefficient is one.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.740
Only finitely many coefficient
lists are possible,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.200
so only finitely many
polynomials can occur.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:57.580
The degree bound and the coefficient
bounds work for every power of z.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.700
Each of these finitely many
polynomials has finitely many roots.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.000
Collect all their roots
into one finite set.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.920
Every positive power of z belongs to
this set. If the set has N elements,

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.800
N plus one powers cannot
all occupy different slots.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.000
Some two actual powers must agree.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:26.380
So z to the a equals z to the b for
some positive integers a less than b.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.380
Since z has magnitude one, it is nonzero.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:36.670
Divide both sides by z to the a. We
obtain z to the b minus a equals one.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.740
The exponent is positive. That is exactly
the definition of a root of unity.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.500
Apply this to z equals Delta of f.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.760
Our two preceding results supply
algebraic integrality and unit

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.860
magnitude for every conjugate, so
Delta has some finite positive order.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.840
We have not yet shown that the order is
odd. The next node brings back the residue

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.680
one information from the local comparisons
to prove that further restriction.
